/*!
\page qtjenny-callbacks.html
\ingroup qtjenny
\meta sortkey {4_callbacks}

\title How callbacks work

\section1 Introduction

There are various situations where you need to implement a callback
when using a Java API. Practical examples of these are callbacks
that listen to content changes via a \c {ContentObserver},
and callbacks that implement Java interfaces such as
\c {Handler.Callback}.

QtJenny allows implementing such callbacks both in the
case of implementing a Java interface, and extending a Java
class, depending on what the Java API uses. Many of the cross-platform
Java APIs that are also provided by Android use callbacks
that are interfaces, but Android also uses abstract base
classes in some situations, a prominent example being \c {ContentObserver}.

\section1 Implementing Java interfaces

The following diagram illustrates the structure and logical division
between different parts of implementing Java interfaces in C++.

\image {../images/qtjenny-callback-interface.drawio.svg} {How it works}

The way it works is that we use a \c {java.reflect.Proxy}, which wraps
a Java interface, and dispatches calls of the interface methods
to the generic \c {invoke()} method of an \c {InvocationHandler}.

The \c {InvocationHandler} interface is implemented by a project-specific
\c {NativeInvocationHandler} (provided by your application), and that implementation calls a native
generic invocation method that is also project-supplied.
The native method calls a generic \c {qt_invoke()} virtual function
in a generated C++ class that mimics the Java interface.

Finally, the user code in the Qt C++ project inherits that generated
class, and overrides the virtual functions in it.

\section1 Extending Java classes

The following diagram illustrates the structure and logical division
between different parts of extending Java classes in C++.

\image {../images/qtjenny-callback-extended-class.drawio.svg} {How it works}

The way it works is that we generate a Java subclass, which then
invokes a generic \c {invoke()} method of a \c {NativeInvocationHandler], which
is a project-supplied Java class.

The \c {NativeInvocationHandler} calls a native
generic invocation method that is also project-supplied.
The native method calls a generic \c {qt_invoke()} virtual function
in a generated C++ class that mimics the Java class.

Finally, the user code in the Qt C++ project inherits that generated
class, and overrides the virtual functions in it.

*/
